  "query_preview": "..Today and Sunday...Drier air has moved into northern\nArizona ahead of a trough. However, while coverage may tick downward\ntoday, enough moisture looks to remain to keep at least isolated \nto scattered coverage for today. Latest hi-res guidance has much \nof the activity along and south/west of the Mogollon Rim. A similar\nstory looks to take hold on Sunday, but with the best coverage \nlooking to shift to eastern Arizona. \n\nWith the trough, 06Z RAP guidance has 0-6 km shear around 25-30 kts. \nTherefore, a couple strong to severe storms will be possible once \nagain this afternoon. Large hail and gusty outflow winds will be the \nprimary hazards with any severe storm. PWATs south of the Mogollon \nRim remain around 0.8-1.0 inches, and 0.5-0.8 inches to the north. \nFlash flooding will be possible, but given that storm motions of \naround 10-15 kts and less of a threat for training storms, the \noverall threat will be localized.\n\nOutside of storms, slightly warmer, but still cool temperatures \nremain over northern Arizona with less and less cloud cover expected \neach day.\n \nMonday through Friday...High pressure slowly begins to retrograde \nand strengthen over the southwest through the coming week. Warming \ntemperatures ensue across northern Arizona as a result, with \nafternoon highs returning to near-normal to slightly (~5F) above \nnormal.\n\nWith monsoonal moisture still in place, precipitation chances look \nto still continue into the coming week. However, as the ridge \nstrengthens, afternoon convection will become suppressed. Therefore, \nmuch of the activity looks to be isolated in nature, and largely \nfocused on the higher terrain.",
